---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';
const path = '/use-cases/email-design';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'use-cases/email-design');
const desc = description(site, 'use-cases/email-design');
const candidates = ["sequenzy", "klaviyo", "omnisend", "shopify-email", "yotpo-email-sms", "drip", "mailchimp", "brevo", "tinyemail", "privy"].map(s => appBySlug(s)!).filter(Boolean);
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<article class="container prose-shopify py-16">
<a href="/use-cases">All use cases</a>
<h1>Design Shopify emails around the next customer decision</h1>
<p>A cart reminder, a product-care lesson and a gift guide should not share the same content hierarchy. Start with what the recipient needs to decide, then choose the smallest useful layout. This is a proposed design and acceptance workflow, not a report of performed inbox tests or merchant conversion results.</p>
<h2>Choose a layout family before opening the editor</h2>
<p>For browse and cart follow-up, try one relevant product, a short reason to return, useful proof and one primary action. For post-purchase, put setup or care instructions ahead of another purchase request. For a promotion, a hero and a small product selection can help comparison, but each extra tile competes with the main action. An editorial VIP note can be mostly text when access details matter more than photography.</p>
<p>Write the message in plain text first. If the offer, product and action cannot be understood without the hero image, move those details into real text. Keep a visible fallback for missing product data. A browser session or abandoned order may refer to an unavailable variant; replacing it silently with another size is not a harmless design choice.</p>
<h2>Separate catalog insertion from live send behavior</h2>
<p><a href="https://support.omnisend.com/en/articles/1061865-use-product-picker-in-product-listing-item">Omnisend documents Product Picker</a> for adding store product details to campaigns and automations. That convenience does not establish that an existing email updates every field at dispatch. Demonstrate what happens after changing a product price, image, stock state or URL between composition and send.</p>
<p>Record the source and refresh behavior of each field. A product name may come from the current catalog while a variant label comes from the original event. Specify whether the message should skip the item, show a safe alternative or cancel when a required field disappears. Previewing a populated example cannot prove the missing-data path works.</p>
<h2>Build reusable content with controlled updates</h2>
<p><a href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/115005413888">Klaviyo documents universal and saved content</a> for reusing blocks and sections. Reuse can reduce repeated editing, but shared updates also require review across the messages that use them. Decide which footer or navigation changes should propagate and which campaign content should stay fixed.</p>
<p>Define typography, spacing, button treatment, header and footer as a short brand specification. Maintain a compact set of approved template families rather than letting each flow owner invent a new header. Keep a change log and nominate a reviewer. Before publishing a shared change, check a long product name, a multi-item order and the simplest text-led message; all three can expose different layout problems.</p>
<h2>Proposed exercise: fashion cart with variant detail</h2>
<p>Create fixtures for a short and long product name, multiple sizes and an unavailable color. Check that image, variant description and destination agree. Compare a single-product message with a product grid only if both answer the same customer question. Preserve the selected variant in the destination where your integration supports it; do not infer this from a generic product link.</p>
<p>Define success as a correct, readable message before measuring clicks. If a later experiment compares layouts, keep offer, audience and timing consistent and record eligible recipients. A click-rate increase alone would not show incremental purchases or improved contribution.</p>
<h2>Proposed exercise: product education with required copy</h2>
<p>For a supplement or care-sensitive product, place approved instructions above the optional shopping action. Give the reviewer the longest approved copy, not just a short placeholder. Check that narrow screens do not bury essential information below an oversized hero. If instructions differ by SKU, validate each supported mapping and define a fallback that does not invent advice.</p>
<p>Store the approval date and copy owner with the template specification. Reusable content helps consistency only when the right version reaches the right products. Avoid promising that an AI-generated layout or a metafield automatically satisfies legal or product-review requirements.</p>
<h2>Proposed exercise: a small store with little design capacity</h2>
<p>Start with one product-led template and one text-led template. Use representative catalog data rather than polished demo imagery. Estimate the time required to update a price, replace an image, correct the footer and restore a previous version. Choose the editor your operator can maintain reliably; advanced branching is little help if routine changes repeatedly break the email.</p>
<p>Evaluate a catalog picker, saved content and custom HTML as different maintenance choices. A picker reduces manual insertion, reusable blocks reduce repeated edits, and custom HTML gives control at the cost of specialist upkeep. Do not rank an editor by the number of templates alone.</p>
<h2>Mobile, image-off and accessibility acceptance</h2>
<p>Send controlled previews to the email clients your audience uses. Check narrow screens, long translated labels, large text settings and dark backgrounds. Make primary actions easy to tap and keep adjacent links apart. Treat any button dimension in your specification as a design target to verify, not a universal rendering guarantee.</p>
<p>Read the message with images hidden. Include meaningful alternative text where images convey information; avoid repeating decorative text unnecessarily. Keep the offer and expiry in selectable text. Use descriptive links, a sensible heading order and sufficient foreground/background contrast. Inspect the logo in both light and dark contexts rather than assuming a transparent asset will stay visible.</p>
<p>Review the plain-text alternative for product names, destinations and a readable action. Automated text extraction can produce a confusing order. Accessibility work aims to make the message understandable; it is not evidence that a particular text-to-image ratio improves inbox placement.</p>
<h2>Release checklist and rollback</h2>
<p>Before approval, record the template version, populated fixtures, missing-field fixtures, client previews, destination checks and reviewer. Confirm footer and unsubscribe behavior through the sending system. Keep marketing and service-message requirements explicit rather than removing navigation or opt-out elements merely to simplify the design.</p>
<p>Schedule a rehearsal after major editor or shared-block changes. Define who can pause an affected flow and restore its prior template. A quarterly review can compare layout families, stale assets and recurring support questions. Retire a layout because it fails a useful requirement or a sound experiment, not because a single campaign had fewer clicks.</p>
<h2>Keep design connected to the workflow</h2>
<p><a href="/use-cases/holiday-gifting">Holiday gifting</a> owns recipient identity and delivery promises; this page owns how those promises remain legible. <a href="/use-cases/discount-strategy">Discount strategy</a> owns offer eligibility and checkout economics; the template must display the approved offer faithfully. A visually attractive button cannot repair an invalid code or a missed cutoff.</p>
<p>For social proof, confirm permission, product relevance and the handling of missing reviews before allocating space to a review module. For SMS, design a separate concise message and destination experience. An email layout does not transfer to a text message, and adding a second channel requires its own eligibility and coordination checks.</p>
<h2>App discovery without a feature verdict</h2>
<p>These are research starting points, not a tested ranking. Ask suppliers to demonstrate your required data, permissions, template behavior and failure cases in the plan you would buy. Confirm current pricing directly; an email editor does not establish SMS support or a shared suppression system.</p>
<ul>{candidates.map(app => <li><a href={`/apps/${app.slug}`}>{app.name}</a></li>)}</ul>
</article>
</BaseLayout>
